<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	  ⚠️ WIRED UP AND SIGNED IN — do not remove CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS from
	  project.pbxproj. Both build configurations set it, and dropping either
	  silently returns the app to the default cap.

	  Verify it survived a build with:
	      codesign -d --entitlements - <App.app> | grep increased-memory

	  Getting here took three things, and the first two were invisible:
	   1. The App ID `com.templetongroup.radiant` DID NOT EXIST. Radiant had
	      been signing against the XC Wildcard App ID (`*`) — hence "iOS Team
	      Provisioning Profile: *" in the failures — and A WILDCARD APP ID
	      CANNOT CARRY CAPABILITIES, so there was no checkbox to find. Fixed by
	      registering it explicitly with Increased Memory Limit enabled.
	   2. Xcode had NO APPLE ID SIGNED IN, so -allowProvisioningUpdates could
	      not generate a profile for the new App ID and kept falling back to the
	      wildcard. `defaults read com.apple.dt.Xcode
	      DVTDeveloperAccountManagerAppleIDLists` was an empty list.
	   3. Then, and only then, CODE_SIGN_ENTITLEMENTS.

	  ⚠️ THIS ENTITLEMENT IS WHAT MAKES THE LARGER MODELS POSSIBLE AT ALL.

	  iOS gives an ordinary app only a fraction of the device's RAM before
	  jetsam kills it — on a 12 GB iPhone that can be under half. A 4 GB model
	  needs about 5 GB resident, so without this the top of the catalogue is
	  permanently "won't run" on hardware that is physically capable of it.

	  With it, iOS raises this process's limit toward the device maximum. It
	  changes what os_proc_available_memory() reports, which is the number the
	  fit labels are computed from — so removing it does not just lose
	  capability, it silently re-labels most of the catalogue.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.developer.kernel.increased-memory-limit</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
